#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175724 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "8c4777a6-d831-54cc-8677-b05ea45b2b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7324" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus follows the logistic growth model\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4P - 0.000\\,01 P^2, \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000$,\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3551/Source/Images/16751776874ece803120a91cd197180d.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dP}{dt} &= 4P - 0.00001P^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.00001P(400000 - P)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that\n\n$$\na = 400\\,000\n$$\n\nHence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000$, and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175798 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/id #uuid "6f96059e-ce3d-5771-b3d8-15044cfd5495" :question/math-academy-id "e-9375" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of some foxes in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n$y' =0.5y - 0.002y^2, \\qquad y(0) = 25$,\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible fox population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3551/Source/Images/23a52670fc85a225d9bd84a9110bd028.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 0.5y - 0.002y^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.002y(250 - y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 250$. Hence, the carrying capacity is $250$, and we conclude that the number of foxes cannot exceed $250$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175799 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "2096869c-2a41-51a5-b2e8-c1039af4d640" :question/math-academy-id "e-9376" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y = y(t)$:\n\n$y' = 2y\\left(60 - \\frac{y}{3} \\right), \\qquad y(0) = 10$\n\nWhat is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)}$?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3551/Source/Images/0b183e2ca94cb514e6aec43fd4576cb7.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2y(60 - \\frac{y}{3}) \\\\\n&= 2y(\\frac{180 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2y}{3}(180 - y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 180$. Hence, the carrying capacity is $180$.\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = 180\n$$"}]}
